699:
The following definitions shall apply in the interpretation and enforcement of this chapter:
Hall County, Georgia means the unincorporated area of Hall County, Georgia.
Household laundry detergent means a laundering cleaning compound in liquid, bar, spray, tablet, flake, powder, or other form used for domestic clothes-cleaning purposes. The term "household laundry detergent" shall not mean:
1. A dishwashing compound, household cleaner, metal cleaner, degreasing compound, commercial cleaner, industrial cleaner, or other substance that is intended to be used for nonlaundry cleaning purposes;
2. A detergent used in dairy, beverage, or food processing cleaning equipment;
3. A phosphorus acid product, including a sanitizer, brightener, acid cleaner, or metal conditioner;
4. A detergent used in hospitals, veterinary hospitals or clinics, or health care facilities or in agricultural production;
5. A detergent used by industry for metal cleaning or conditioning;
6. A detergent manufactured, stored or distributed for use or sale outside the county;
7. A detergent used in any laboratory, including a biological laboratory, research facility, chemical laboratory, and engineering laboratory; or
8. A detergent used in a commercial laundry that provides laundry services for a hospital, health care facility or veterinary hospital.
Person means any person, firm, partnership or corporation.
Phosphorous means elemental phosphorus.
(Res. of 10-23-89, § 2; Code 1991, § 8.32.020)
Cross reference— Definitions and rules of construction generally, § 1.20.010 et seq.

707:
Nothing in this chapter shall be construed to prohibit the following:
A. The wholesale or retail sale of fireworks for use in a public exhibition or public display and the transportation of fireworks for such use, provided that any person selling at wholesale or retail or transporting fireworks for such use must have a duplicate copy of the permit which has been issued by the local fire authority to a person, firm, corporation, association, or partnership which has been authorized to hold a public exhibition or display;
B. Use by railroads or other transportation agencies of fireworks specifically designed and intended for signal purposes or illumination;
C. The sale or use of blank cartridges for a show or theater or for signal or ceremonial purposes in athletic or sports events or for use by military or police organizations; or
D. The manufacture of any fireworks not prohibited by Congress or any federal agency; the possession, transportation, and storage of any such fireworks by any manufacturer thereof; the storage of certain such fireworks by a nonmanufacturer in accordance with the provisions of O.C.G.A. § 25-10-3.1; the possession, transportation, or distribution of any such fireworks to a distributor located outside this state; the sale of such fireworks by any such manufacturer to a distributor located outside this state; or the possession and transportation of such fireworks by any manufacturer or contractor or common carrier from the point of manufacture within this state to any point outside this state.
(Res. of 11-28-88, § 1(B); Code 1991, § 8.36.040; Res. of 1-23-25(2), § 1(Exh. A))

714:
No person shall cause, suffer, allow or permit open burning at any place in the unincorporated areas of the county except as follows:
A. Carrying out recognized agricultural procedures necessary for production or harvesting of crops;
B. The burning over of any forest land by the owners of such land;
C. For recreational purposes or cooking food for immediate human consumption;
D. Fires set for purposes of training fire-fighting personnel when authorized by the appropriate governmental entity and the guidelines set forth by the fire marshal are strictly observed;
E. Operation of devices using open flames such as tar kettles, blow torches, welding torches, portable heaters and other flame-making equipment;
F. Setting and maintenance by contractors and tradesman of miscellaneous small fires necessary to such activities as street paving work, and installation or repair of utilities, provided that such fires are kept small in size, that no smoke emissions exceed 40 percent opacity, and that local ordinances and regulations do not prohibit such actions;
G. Disposal of all packing materials previously containing explosives, in accordance with U.S. Department of Labor Safety regulations.
(Res. of 11-28-88, § 1(C); Code 1991, § 8.40.010)

715:
The following shall be permitted if adequate disposal facilities for the particular combustible materials involved are not reasonably available:
A. Reduction of leaves on the premises on which they fall by the person in control of the premises;
B. Destruction of combustible demolition or construction materials either on sight or transported to a burning facility approved by the fire marshal;
C. Disposal of tree limbs from storm damage;
D. For weed abatement, disease, and pest prevention;
E. Open burning in other than predominately residential areas for the purpose of land clearing or construction or right-of-way maintenance provided the following conditions are met:
1. Prevailing winds at the time of the burning are away from the major portion of the area's population;
2. The location of the burning is at least 1,000 feet from any dwelling located in a predominately residential area;
3. The amount of dirt on or in the material being burned is minimized;
4. Heavy oils, asphaltic materials, items containing natural or synthetic rubber, or any materials other than plant growth are not being burned;
5. Not more than one pile 60 feet by 60 feet, or equivalent, is being burned within a nine-acre area at one time;
provided, however, that in no event shall the open burning of more than 100 cubic yards per day of material described in subsection E of this section be permitted unless the person performing such burning shall have first given two days' written notice of the time and place of such burning to the fire marshal.
(Res. of 11-28-88, § 1(C); Code 1991, § 8.40.020)

740:
(a) The public officer may determine, under existing ordinances, that a dwelling, building or structure is unfit for human habitation or is unfit for its current commercial, industrial, or business use if he finds that conditions exist in such building, dwelling, or structure which are dangerous or injurious to the health, safety, or morals of the occupants of such dwelling, building, or structure; of the occupants of neighborhood dwellings, buildings, or structures; or of other residents of Hall County. Such conditions may include the following (without limiting the generality of the foregoing):
(1) Defects therein increasing the hazards of fire, accidents, or other calamities;
(2) Lack of adequate ventilation, light, or sanitary facilities;
(3) Dilapidation;
(4) Disrepair;
(5) Structural defects; and
(6) Uncleanliness.
(b) The public officer may determine, under existing ordinances, that a dwelling, building or structure is vacant, dilapidated, and being used in connection with the commission of drug crimes upon personal observation or report of a law enforcement agency and evidence of drug crimes being committed.
(Res. of 7-13-06, § 1)

741:
The public officer may exercise such powers as may be necessary or convenient to carry out and effectuate the purpose of this chapter, including the following powers in addition to others granted in this chapter:
(1) To investigate the dwelling conditions in the unincorporated area of Hall County in order to determine which dwellings, buildings or structures therein are unfit for human habitation or are unfit for current commercial, industrial, or business use or are vacant, dilapidated, and being used in connection with the commission of drug crimes;
(2) To administer oaths and affirmations, to examine witnesses, and to receive evidence;
(3) To enter upon premises for the purpose of making examinations; provided, however, that such entries shall be made in such manner as to cause the least possible inconvenience to the persons in possession;
(4) To appoint and fix the duties of such officers, agents, and employees as he deems necessary to carry out the purposes of this chapter; and
(5) To delegate any of his functions and powers under this chapter to such officers and agents as he may designate.
(Res. of 7-13-06, § 1)

799:
This chapter shall not apply to:
A. A passenger of a vehicle in which the driver is operating the vehicle pursuant to a contract to provide transportation for passengers and such driver holds a valid chauffeur's license pursuant to Georgia law or [the law of] any other state;
B. A passenger of a bus in which the driver holds a valid chauffeur's license pursuant to Georgia law or [the law of] any other state;
C. A passenger of a self-contained motor home. Self-contained motor home means every motor home vehicle designed, used, or maintained primarily as a mobile dwelling, mobile office or mobile commercial space. This exemption shall not apply to a passenger of a self-contained motor home who is seated in the front of the mobile home parallel to the operator of the motor home.
(Res. of 10-9-89, § 5; Code 1991, § 9.04.050)

867:
Except when necessary to avoid conflict with other traffic, or in compliance with law or directions of a law enforcement officer or official traffic control device, no person shall stop, stand or park a vehicle:
A. On a sidewalk;
B. Within an intersection;
C. On a designated crosswalk;
D. At any place where official signs prohibit stopping or parking;
E. In any designated parking space for a time period in excess of the time period posted on the official sign marking the parking space, which time period may not be restricted to less than 15 minutes;
F. In any designated parking space when the person is not transacting business in the county courthouse or its annex or serving as a juror or witness in the courthouse;
G. In any parking space which is designated by an official sign for the use of county officials, employees or jurors, unless they qualify as a designated party for the space.
(Res. of 2-27-78, § 1; Code 1991, § 10.24.010)

898:
Except when necessary to avoid conflict with other traffic, or in compliance with directions of a law enforcement officer or official traffic control device, no person shall stop, stand or park a vehicle:
A. In any aisle or driveway outside marked parking spaces;
B. Within an intersection connecting parking levels;
C. On a designated crosswalk;
D. At any place where official signs prohibit stopping or parking;
E. In any parking space which is designated by an official sign for the use of county government officials, employees, jurors, or U.S. government employees unless they qualify as a designated party for the space.
(Res. of 8-28-89, § 1; Code 1991, § 10.36.010)

921:
Owners of property which abuts a road adjacent to a publicly maintained road may petition the board of commissioners for acceptance of the road into the system by making application to the county's department of Public Works and Utilities (hereinafter "department").
A. In all cases, the application shall contain the following:
1. The name, address, telephone number, and signature of each owner adjacent to the road which is proposed for acceptance;
2. An adequate description of the road sought to be accepted into the county's system that reasonably describes it for purposes of land records, such as by legal description, survey, plat, or other sufficient methods;
3. Proof of express dedication of the road to public use evidenced by a right-of-way deed, properly executed, witnessed, and notarized in accordance with state law and acceptable for recording, and conveying to the county the minimum necessary right-of-way required for the class of road involved according to then existing standards, or proof of implied dedication as evidenced by permitted open, continuous, and notorious use by the public, or both;
4. Proof of express acceptance by the board of commissioners of such dedication evidenced by entry on the minutes of the commission or proof of implied acceptance of such dedication by acts of the county constituting maintenance, improvement, or control over the road;
5. Proof that the road is a road dedicated to the public and open to vehicular traffic, and is not a private road, a developer's road, or a driveway;
6. Proof that there is an average of one house per 500 linear feet of road; and
7. Proof that the road serves a public purpose.
B. As to roads which were previously publicly maintained roads, the application shall also contain:
1. Proof that the road was previously a publicly maintained road, maintained by the county, which burden of proof shall be on the applicant(s);
2. If the road needs to be upgraded to a condition that allows for county maintenance, a proposal for the payment of fees sufficient to cover the costs of the necessary upgrade. The department of public works and utilities shall determine the amount of the fees to be paid, and the department's determination must reflect the cost of providing the necessary upgrading to the road. To the extent that the creation of a special taxing district is necessary in order to ensure that the fees are paid in full, the county is authorized to create such a district and assess it accordingly. In such an instance, the proposal for the payment of fees shall request that the county create such a district, and provide all of the information on the properties and property owners covered by the district that is necessary to implement accurate and timely assessments.
C. As to roads which have never been part of the system, or as to which the applicant(s) cannot meet the burden of proof required in subsection B of this section, the application shall also contain proof that the road meets minimum standards for acceptance into the system for that class of road, as provided by the existing standards.
(Res. of 3-23-23(1), § 1(Exh. A))

940:
Driveway angles [shall be as follows]:
A. Residential, 60 to 90 degrees, or parallel to property line;
B. Apartments, 70 to 90 degrees for two-way driveways or 45 to 90 degrees for one-way streets;
C. Commercial, 70 to 90 degrees for two-way driveways or 45 to 90 degrees for one-way on divided roadway or 60 to 90 degrees for one-way ramp on non-divided roadway;
D. Industrial, 90 degrees, or as close as practicable, for two-way, or 45 degrees, one-way access on one-way street;
E. Rural, land access only, 60 to 90 degrees.
(Res. of 9-14-81, § 6(B)(2); Code 1991, § 12.12.090)

945:
The length, width and shape of corner island areas will vary for different locations. The angle of intersection, angle of driveways, width of the right-of-way on both approaches, channelization radii, and other conditions will influence the location of driveways at intersections. The location and angle of an approach in relation to the highway intersection shall be such that a vehicle leaving the service facility may be merged in the lane of traffic moving in the desired direction before crossing the intersection, and that a vehicle entering the facility from the intersection may do so in an orderly and safe manner with a minimum of interference to through traffic.
The following conditions may be applicable in most instances:
A. No driveway shall be allowed to encroach upon pavement edge radii.
B. The following minimum distances from the intersection right-of-way line (C) shall apply where there is no conflict with the foregoing conditions:
1. Traffic volume A.D.T. of 1,500 or less, 20 feet;
2. Traffic volume A.D.T. over 1,500 but less than 10,000, 35 feet;
3. Traffic volume A.D.T. over 10,000 but less than 15,000, 50 feet;
4. Traffic volume A.D.T. over 15,000, 65 feet.
The public works director shall have the authority to increase these distances if in his opinion such action is necessary for the protection of traffic. The public works director may also modify these requirements if justifiable on the basis of site conditions.
(Res. of 9-14-81, § 6(B)(7); Code 1991, § 12.12.140)

953:
Construction of common access driveways shall conform to the following minimum standards. Please see Figure 6 for a cross-section detail:
A. Easement width, 30 feet, 30 feet radius easement in cul-de-sac;
B. Roadbed width 20 feet;
C. Shoulder width, two feet;
D. Base width, 18 feet;
E. Pavement width, 16 feet;
F. Minimum radius at cul-de-sac transition and intersections, 20 feet;
G. Pavement type:
1. Plant mix, one inch thick over graded aggregate four inches thick (except within the public right-of-way, which shall be six inches thick);
2. Concrete, four inches thick (except within the public right-of-way, which shall be six inches thick);
H. If a common access driveway terminates in a dead end, the termination shall be either a cul-de-sac with a paved radius of 25 feet, or another termination method that allows for vehicles to turn around safely and is acceptable to Hall County Engineering;
I. If a common access driveway is a cul-de-sac, the maximum length of the cul-de-sac shall be 1,000 feet;
J. Individual driveways joining the common driveway must be placed entirely on the property that they serve;
K. Individual driveways joining the common driveway may only serve one lot;
L. Ditches: Front slope, maximum 2:1 ratio; rear slope, maximum 2:1 ratio; 1 ½ foot minimum depth;
M. Maximum finished driveway grade, 17 percent; driveway grades up to 20 percent may be approved by the county engineer in extreme conditions;
N. Pavement crown, ¼-inch per foot;
O. All slopes and shoulders to be grassed;
P. Minimum crossdrain pipe size, 18 inches;
Q. The driveway must intersect the public road at an angle of 65 degrees or greater;
R. The driveway must enter the public road at least 65 feet from any intersection of another common access driveway as measured from centerline to centerline. The driveway must enter the public road at least 65 feet from another public road intersection as measured from the centerline of the common access driveway to the closest point of right-of-way intersection;
S. Intersection sight distance shall be as specified in the sight distance table in section 16.60.090 of this Code.
(Res. of 6-8-87, § 2; Code 1991, § 12.12.220; Res. of 11-22-00(1), § 2)
